1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,067
On a handmade Neocities page, someone meant to

2
00:00:03,067 --> 00:00:06,135
put this picture next to this note. Can you

3
00:00:06,135 --> 00:00:09,202
really arrange a page like this by writing code?

4
00:00:09,852 --> 00:00:13,639
Yes. The maker writes relationships. The browser
chooses

5
00:00:13,639 --> 00:00:17,427
the coordinates that satisfy them on each
screen.

6
00:00:18,077 --> 00:00:20,909
This is our Moon Snail Post Office,

7
00:00:20,909 --> 00:00:23,742
opened in a real browser. Watch the illustration

8
00:00:23,742 --> 00:00:26,574
move above the note as the window narrows.

9
00:00:26,574 --> 00:00:29,406
The little stamp stays attached to the postcard.

10
00:00:30,056 --> 00:00:34,245
A PNG screenshot freezes the layout and text. An
SVG

11
00:00:34,245 --> 00:00:38,434
can stay crisp and may contain selectable text,
but scaling

12
00:00:38,434 --> 00:00:42,622
a fixed composition is not the same as HTML
reflowing.

13
00:00:43,272 --> 00:00:46,819
Start in a plain text editor. Save index

14
00:00:46,819 --> 00:00:50,365
dot html. HTML describes the things on the page,

15
00:00:50,365 --> 00:00:53,911
and the order they belong in. A separate

16
00:00:53,911 --> 00:00:57,457
style sheet describes how they look and fit
together.

17
00:00:58,107 --> 00:01:01,441
Here's the actual postcard structure. The
section holds

18
00:01:01,441 --> 00:01:04,775
a snail image and a note. The image

19
00:01:04,775 --> 00:01:08,109
source names its file; the alternative text
describes

20
00:01:08,109 --> 00:01:11,444
the picture for someone who cannot see it.

21
00:01:12,094 --> 00:01:15,651
Open the page locally in a browser. Here

22
00:01:15,651 --> 00:01:19,208
we use a small local server. The browser reads

23
00:01:19,208 --> 00:01:22,765
the HTML and the linked style sheet, then draws

24
00:01:22,765 --> 00:01:26,322
the result. You're editing instructions, not
painting a screenshot.

25
00:01:26,972 --> 00:01:30,123
With no special layout rule, blocks normally

26
00:01:30,123 --> 00:01:33,274
follow one another. The note comes after the

27
00:01:33,274 --> 00:01:36,426
picture. That ordinary flow is our starting
point.

28
00:01:37,076 --> 00:01:39,921
A drag might say put the snail at

29
00:01:39,921 --> 00:01:42,766
this coordinate. Our author means something more
useful:

30
00:01:42,766 --> 00:01:45,611
put the picture beside the note when there

31
00:01:45,611 --> 00:01:48,457
is room, but keep both readable when there
isn't.

32
00:01:49,107 --> 00:01:52,430
The postcard is the parent box. Its grid rule

33
00:01:52,430 --> 00:01:55,754
arranges the image and the note as children.
This

34
00:01:55,754 --> 00:01:59,078
columns rule fits as many columns as it can,

35
00:01:59,078 --> 00:02:02,401
aiming for a minimum of two hundred sixty pixels
each.

36
00:02:03,051 --> 00:02:07,084
Wide enough for two? The snail sits beside

37
00:02:07,084 --> 00:02:11,116
the note. Too narrow? They stack, picture first.

38
00:02:11,116 --> 00:02:15,148
The browser recalculates coordinates while
keeping the author's relationship.

39
00:02:15,798 --> 00:02:19,125
The stamp has a different job. Position

40
00:02:19,125 --> 00:02:22,452
relative makes the postcard a reference box.
Position

41
00:02:22,452 --> 00:02:25,779
absolute, top ten, right twelve pins the stamp

42
00:02:25,779 --> 00:02:29,106
to that box, not to the browser window.

43
00:02:29,756 --> 00:02:33,025
Now let's make one deliberate change: more

44
00:02:33,025 --> 00:02:36,294
space between the snail and the note. In

45
00:02:36,294 --> 00:02:39,563
the postcard rule, change gap from twenty four

46
00:02:39,563 --> 00:02:42,832
pixels to sixty. Save the file, then refresh.

47
00:02:43,482 --> 00:02:47,345
Here is the real browser before and after. The

48
00:02:47,345 --> 00:02:51,209
gap grew, and the two columns became narrower.
One

49
00:02:51,209 --> 00:02:55,072
edit changed their relationship; neither picture
needed a new coordinate.

50
00:02:55,722 --> 00:02:59,339
To investigate, View Source shows the original
HTML

51
00:02:59,339 --> 00:03:02,955
loaded for the page. Inspector shows the live

52
00:03:02,955 --> 00:03:06,571
document tree and computed layout. Find the
postcard

53
00:03:06,571 --> 00:03:10,187
parent, its image and note, and the stamp.

54
00:03:10,837 --> 00:03:14,360
Your turn. Predict what will happen if you

55
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:17,883
change the postcard gap to twelve pixels. Save,
refresh,

56
00:03:17,883 --> 00:03:21,405
and check. Then narrow the browser. Where does

57
00:03:21,405 --> 00:03:24,928
the note go, and what happens to the stamp?

58
00:03:25,578 --> 00:03:29,484
To share your page, upload index dot html, style

59
00:03:29,484 --> 00:03:33,390
dot css, and the two image files to a static

60
00:03:33,390 --> 00:03:37,297
host. Neocities also has a browser editor for
your files.

61
00:03:37,947 --> 00:03:41,641
A free Neocities address follows the pattern
site name

62
00:03:41,641 --> 00:03:45,336
dot neocities dot org. The host serves your
files, and

63
00:03:45,336 --> 00:03:49,031
each visitor's browser lays them out for its own
screen.

64
00:03:49,681 --> 00:03:53,170
The maker chose the relationships: snail beside
note

65
00:03:53,170 --> 00:03:56,659
when it fits, stamp on postcard, pictures in

66
00:03:56,659 --> 00:04:00,148
a gallery. The browser does the measuring.
That's

67
00:04:00,148 --> 00:04:03,637
how typed instructions can make a page feel
handmade.
